<% survey = @show.complexity_survey %>

<h2>Question</h2>
<p>
  A world that crosses into self-replication has found something. The programme can now say
  when it crossed, <%= link_to "whether it stays there", finding_path("emergence-can-be-left") %>
  and <%= link_to "what a copy costs it", finding_path("copy-cost-adaptation") %> — but not
  whether what it found keeps growing. That is the open-endedness question in its plainest
  form: after emergence, does the dominant replicator get more complicated as the epochs go
  by, or does the world settle on one recipe and elaborate nothing further? Every richer
  substrate this programme goes on to try is worth trying only against an answer to that on
  the substrate it already has.
</p>

<h2>Method</h2>
<p>
  Each sample already picks out a <strong>dominant replicator</strong>: the most populous
  tape among the <span class="mono">top_k</span> tested that passes the replicator test, the
  tape <span class="mono">copy_cost</span> is priced on. The engine now reads that same tape
  two more ways. <span class="mono">dominant_compressed_len</span> is its length in bytes
  after zlib — the compressor <span class="mono">compress_ratio</span> already uses, so a
  tape and the world it sits in are measured on one scale, and a tape of filler with a short
  program in it reads short however long the tape is.
  <span class="mono">dominant_instruction_count</span> is how many of its bytes the run's own
  instruction set executes, so an ablation sweep counts only what its interpreter would run.
  Both are null when no tested tape replicates. The hand-written replicator in the engine's
  test suite reads <strong>36 bytes and 15 instructions</strong>, which is what pins them.
</p>
<p>
  This page then reads the series back out of the lab: every transitioned run, whichever
  sweep queued it, its samples at or after its own crossing epoch, in epoch order. A run is
  counted as growing when its last recorded length is above its first post-transition one,
  shrinking when it is below, flat when the two are equal. Counts of directions, nothing
  fitted.
</p>

<h2>Result</h2>
<% if survey.any? %>
  <p>
    <strong>
      <% if survey.compared_count.zero? %>
        No run has two readings of the length yet, so nothing can be compared.
      <% elsif survey.grew_count > survey.shrank_count + survey.flat_count %>
        More transitioned worlds end with a more complicated replicator than end with a
        simpler or an unchanged one.
      <% elsif survey.grew_count.zero? %>
        No transitioned world in the lab ends with a more complicated replicator than it
        started with: on this substrate, complexity plateaus after emergence.
      <% else %>
        Growth is the exception rather than the rule: most transitioned worlds end no more
        complicated than they started.
      <% end %>
    </strong>
    The lab has <%= pluralize survey.transitioned_count, "terminal run" %> that crossed, with
    a complexity reading after the crossing in <%= survey.measured_count %> of them, across
    <%= pluralize survey.sweeps.size, "sweep" %>.
    <% if survey.compared_count.positive? %>
      Among the <%= pluralize survey.compared_count, "run" %> with at least two readings, the
      split is <%= survey.grew_count %> ending above their first post-transition length,
      <%= survey.shrank_count %> below it and <%= survey.flat_count %> on it.
    <% end %>
  </p>

  <div id="complexity-runs" class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region"
       aria-label="Complexity of the dominant replicator in every transitioned run that recorded one">
    <table class="data-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Sweep</th>
          <th>Run</th>
          <th class="numeric">Transition epoch</th>
          <th class="numeric">Readings</th>
          <th class="numeric">First length (epoch)</th>
          <th class="numeric">Last length (epoch)</th>
          <th class="numeric">Largest</th>
          <th class="numeric">Instructions, first → last</th>
          <th>Direction</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <% survey.table_rows.each do |row| %>
          <tr>
            <td><%= link_to row.experiment.name, experiment_path(row.experiment) %></td>
            <td><%= link_to "seed #{row.seed}", run_path(row.run) %></td>
            <td class="numeric mono"><%= number_with_delimiter row.transition_epoch %></td>
            <td class="numeric mono"><%= number_with_delimiter row.readings %></td>
            <td class="numeric mono">
              <%= number_with_delimiter row.first_length %>
              (<%= number_with_delimiter row.first_epoch %>)
            </td>
            <td class="numeric mono">
              <%= number_with_delimiter row.last_length %>
              (<%= number_with_delimiter row.last_epoch %>)
            </td>
            <td class="numeric mono"><%= number_with_delimiter row.max_length %></td>
            <td class="numeric mono">
              <%= number_with_delimiter row.first_instructions %> →
              <%= number_with_delimiter row.last_instructions %>
            </td>
            <td>
              <% if row.grew? %>
                <span class="badge badge-success">more complicated</span>
              <% elsif row.shrank? %>
                <span class="badge badge-warning">simpler</span>
              <% elsif row.flat? %>
                <span class="badge badge-info">unchanged</span>
              <% else %>
                <span class="text-muted">one reading</span>
              <% end %>
            </td>
          </tr>
        <% end %>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="text-sm text-muted">
    One row per transitioned run whose samples carry a complexity reading at or after its
    crossing, grouped by sweep. Lengths are bytes of the run's dominant replicator after
    zlib at that sample; "Largest" is the biggest the run ever recorded.
    <% if survey.capped? %>
      The table stops at <%= pluralize Findings::ShowPage::MAX_TRANSITIONS, "row" %> and
      leaves <%= pluralize survey.rows_omitted, "further run" %> out; every one of them is
      counted in the figures above.
    <% end %>
  </p>
<% else %>
  <p class="text-muted">
    No run in this database has recorded the complexity of its dominant replicator yet. The
    observable is new, and a run only carries it from the moment the lab's runners started
    measuring it, so this table fills in as the worlds now on the clock reach their next
    sample — and stays empty for every run that finished before the engine could read a tape
    this way.
  </p>
<% end %>

<h2>What this does not claim</h2>
<p>
  <strong>Compressed length is size, not ability.</strong> A tape that grows by a hundred
  bytes of junk it never executes reads as more complicated here, and a tape that learns to
  do something new in the bytes it already had reads as unchanged. Length and instruction
  count bound the recipe rather than describe it; nothing on this page opens a tape and says
  what it does.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>"Dominant replicator" is a tape, not a lineage.</strong> The reading is taken off
  the most populous tape that passes the replicator test at that sample. A length that jumps
  between two samples can be one colony elaborating itself — or a different, longer
  replicator taking over, which is a different sentence, and this page cannot tell the two
  apart.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>A plateau here is a plateau at these budgets.</strong> Every series is censored at
  both ends: no run recorded a complexity before this observable existed, so each starts
  wherever the lab first ran the new engine rather than at the world's crossing, and ends
  wherever that run's budget ended. A world that would have elaborated at epoch 200 000 and
  was stopped at 60 000 reads flat.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>This is not the open-endedness sweep.</strong> The runs above transitioned in
  sweeps built to answer other questions — mutation rate, world size, locality, interaction
  budget — none of them designed or funded to watch a replicator grow. A count of directions
  over such a set describes the set.
</p>

<h2>What next</h2>
<p>
  The series has to accumulate before it can be read: the runs on the clock need to sample
  under the new engine for long enough to give more than a couple of readings each. After
  that, the reading that would really settle the matter is per lineage rather than per
  dominant tape, so one colony can be watched growing; and the answer this page eventually
  gives is the baseline against which the substrate bets of the design record — a richer
  instruction set, an environment with something to exploit — are worth running at all. If
  complexity plateaus here and plateaus there too, the substrate was not what was limiting.
</p>
